The page is a working sheet in the run of notes on cartes/dessins (previous page: 'cartes trouées', D_n^*, combinatorial polyhedra Π_n) and on Galois/Kummer formalism (next page: bitorsors). It has three blocks.

(1) Top left: the Nielsen–Schreier index formula. L is a free group on d generators and L' ⊂ L a subgroup of index n, free on d' generators, with d'−1 = n(d−1). Topologically, L ≅ π1(X) for a graph X (a bouquet of circles) with Euler characteristic 1−d. L' ≅ π1(X'), where X' → X is the n-sheeted covering, so χ(X') = 1−d' = n(1−d). The check '2 = 2·1' is d=2, n=2, giving d'=3, and the bouquet of three circles is drawn.

(2) Top right: the shaded triangle with vertices 0, 1, ∞ is half of P^1 minus {0,1,∞}, i.e. the basic 'carte' of dessins d'enfants. Its fundamental group π is generated by ρ0, ρ1, ρ∞ with ρ0ρ1ρ∞ = 1. π^+ is the extension of π by Z/2 coming from complex conjugation (the orientation-reversing symmetry, as in the cartographic groups C_2 ⊂ C_2^+ of the Esquisse). The involution is ρ0 ↦ ρ0^{-1}, ρ∞ ↦ ρ∞^{-1}, ρ1 ↦ ρ∞ρ0. This is the standard description of complex conjugation on π1(P^1−{0,1,∞}) for a real base point, up to an inner automorphism or convention.

(3) Main block: a Morita/Diaconescu-type description of colimit-preserving functors out of a classifying topos. For a group π, Ens(π) is the category of π-sets. Functors F: Ens(π) → C that commute with (inductive) limits correspond to objects X of C with a (right) π-action:
- F ↦ F(π_s), the value on the regular π-set;
- X ↦ F_X : E ↦ X ×_π E, the contracted product.
The right adjoint is Y ↦ Hom(X,Y), via Hom_C(X ×_π E, Y) ≅ Hom_π(E, Hom(X,Y)). Writing a π-set as E ≅ ⊔_i π/H_i gives X ×_π E = ⊔_i X/H_i, which shows F_X is determined by the orbit quotients X/H_i.
The special case C = Ens(π') (≅ the second target C' on the sheet) leads to the next page:
- colimit-preserving functors correspond to (π',π)-bisets;
- F is left exact iff X is a right π-torsor;
- F is an equivalence iff X is a (π',π)-bitorsor;
- hence Équiv(Ens(π), Ens(π')) ≅ Bit(π',π), with inversion of equivalences matching inversion of bitorsors.
The purpose is evidently to set up the 'Galois-Kummer' formalism: actions of π^+ or Galois groups on categories of coverings (dessins) are to be read as bitorsors and outer actions.
